7, lines 326--327). The shift by 16 keeps","+\\(\\ln\\ln\\) defined and positive at every \\(n \\ge 0\\) (\\(\\ln 16 = 2.77\\)),","+so the theorem can be stated for all \\(n\\) with no proviso. Then","+\\begin{quote}","+\\texttt{runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)\\^{}d * 2\\^{}(A * runtimeScale n)}","+\\end{quote}","+(lines 136--139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent","+power. We write \\(\\rhon\\) for \\texttt{runtimeScale n} throughout the","+paper.","+","+\\subsection{The theorem, and the order of its quantifiers}","+","+Lines 194--217 state, for every \\(k\\) with \\(2 \\le k\\):","+\\[","+\\begin{array}{l}","+\\exists\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\ \\mathtt{countTape},\\ \\mathtt{answerTape},\\","+  C, A, S \\in \\mathbb{R},\\ d, e \\in \\mathbb{N},\\\\[2pt]","+\\quad 0 < C \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < A \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < S \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < d \\ \\wedge\\ 0 < e\\ \\wedge\\\\[2pt]","+\\quad \\forall\\, n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\forall\\, F : \\mathtt{CNF}\\ (\\mathtt{Fin}\\ n),\\","+  \\mathtt{WidthAtMost}\\ k\\ F \\;\\rightarrow\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad \\exists\\ \\mathtt{time} \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ \\mathtt{final} : \\mathtt{LocatedCfg}\\ \\mathtt{machine},\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\mathtt{Trace}\\ (\\mathtt{locatedStep}\\ \\mathtt{machine})\\ \\mathtt{time}\\","+  (\\mathtt{locatedInit}\\ \\mathtt{machine}\\ (\\mathtt{encodeCNF}\\ F))\\ \\mathtt{final}","+  \\quad\\text{(lines 201--202)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the count tape holds \\texttt{countWord (count F)}}","+  \\quad\\text{(203--204)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the answer tape holds the bit \\([0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]\\)}","+  \\quad\\text{(205--206)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\text{the halting state carries that bit}","+  \\quad\\text{(207)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{toNat}\\ (\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)) = \\mathtt{count}\\ F","+  \\quad\\text{(208)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ |\\mathtt{countWord}\\ (\\mathtt{count}\\ F)| \\le n + 1","+  \\quad\\text{(209)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\mathtt{time} \\le C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\\ L = \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F","+  \\quad\\text{(210--211)}\\\\[2pt]","+\\qquad\\quad \\wedge\\ \\forall\\ \\text{prefix of the trace},\\","+  \\mathtt{visitedSpace} \\le S\\,(L+1)^e","+  \\quad\\text{(212--217).}","+\\end{array}","+\\]","+Three features of the order matter.","+","+\\emph{The machine and the five constants come after \\(k\\) and before","+\\(n\\) and \\(F\\).} They may depend on \\(k\\) and on nothing else; inside","+the universal quantifier only \\texttt{time} and \\texttt{final} are","+chosen. So for each \\(k\\) one fixed finite machine serves every formula","+of every size, and \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are the same for all of them: the","+standard \\(O_k(1)\\) reading. The table's size is \\(O_k(1)\\) in the same","+sense, with no explicit function of \\(k\\).","+","+\\emph{There is no lower bound on \\(n\\).} The manuscript's Theorem~1.1","+and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\) (p.~2, lines 56--57;","+p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's auxiliary","+\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported","+theorem does not. The \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that possible.","+","+\\emph{The constants are existential.} The statement asserts that values","+of \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) exist; it names none. The proof obtains them from","+existential lemmas and never evaluates them: at the point where the","+large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to","+\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;","+\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and","+the degree and the polynomial constants are built the same way. So the","+proof as written has \\(A \\ge 257\\), and no closed value of any of the","+five. The witness machine is explicit in one respect: it is compiled","+from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with","+\\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is","+a finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. Section~\\ref{sec:proof}","+is the inspection of the proof; this section only reads what the","+statement fixes and what it leaves open.","+","+\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}","+","+Everything the title says rests on one function. With \\(y = \\ln(n+16)\\),","+\\[","+  \\frac{\\rhon}{n} \\;=\\; \\frac{(\\ln y)^2}{\\sqrt{y}},","+\\]","+which rises from \\(0.64\\) at \\(n = 1\\), crosses \\(1\\) between","+\\(n = 49\\) and \\(n = 50\\), peaks at \\(16/e^2 = 2.165\\) where","+\\(\\ln y = 4\\), i.e.\\ \\(y = e^4\\), \\(n = e^{e^4} - 16 \\approx 10^{23.7}\\),","+and returns below \\(1\\) only where \\((\\ln y)^2 = \\sqrt{y}\\) again, at","+\\(y = 5503.7\\), i.e.\\ \\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\).","+Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and","+Figures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them; every","+number is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}","+(run under \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and","+computes in \\(y\\), so that no \\(n\\) beyond floating-point range is ever","+formed). The crossing at 50 is a fact about integers:","+\\(\\rho(49)/49 = 0.9994\\) and \\(\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027\\). In the limit","+\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\), since \\((\\ln y)^2/\\sqrt{y} \\to 0\\): the exponent is","+\\(o(n)\\), and that is a theorem in our companion Lean","+\\leanref{rho-little-o}. Both facts are true at once, and the second is","+the one the title is about: the function is \\(o(n)\\), and it is above","+\\(n\\) on every size anyone will ever write down.","+","+\\begin{table}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\caption{The verified exponent relative to \\(n\\). \\(\\rhon = n(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}\\),","+the comparator's \\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms.","+The verified bound is \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\); at \\(A = 1\\) its exponential","+factor is \\(2^{(\\rhon/n)\\cdot n}\\), against \\(2^n\\) for trying every assignment.","+Regenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}","+\\label{tab:runtime-scale}","+\\input{tables/runtime_scale}","+\\end{table}","+","+\\begin{figure}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]","+\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}","+\\caption{The verified exponent against brute force. Top: \\(\\rhon/n\\) over","+the whole range, the axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from \\(n = 10\\) to","+\\(n = 10^{3000}\\); the dashed level is \\(2^n\\); beneath it the manuscript's two","+sharper exponents, \\(n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (Theorem~1.1, general) and","+\\(n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}\\) (fixed clause density), which are not in the Lean","+library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the","+practical range \\(n = 10\\) to \\(10^6\\), with \\(\\rhon/n\\) marked at","+\\(n = 50, 125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6\\). All curves at the constant \\(A = 1\\);","+the verified \\(A\\) is unknown. Regenerate:","+\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}","+\\label{fig:central}","+\\end{figure}","+","+\\begin{figure}[htbp]","+\\centering","+\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}","+\\caption{\\(\\rhon/n\\) alone, on a logarithmic axis in \\(\\log_{10} n\\) from","+\\(n = 10\\) to \\(n = 10^{2500}\\), with the level \\(1\\) (brute force, \\(2^n\\)),","+the first integer above the level (\\(n = 50\\)), the peak (\\(16/e^2 = 2.17\\)","+at \\(\\ln(n+16) = e^4\\), \\(n \\approx 10^{23.7}\\)), the return below the level","+(\\(n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}\\)), and the practical range","+\\(50 \\le n \\le 10^6\\) shaded. Regenerate:","+\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}","+\\label{fig:runtime-scale}","+\\end{figure}","+","+\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}","+","+The title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all \\(2^n\\)","+assignments at every practical size. Read against the statement, that is","+the following, and nothing more.","+","+\\begin{enumerate}","+\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The","+  theorem says the machine halts within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps.","+  It does not say how many steps the machine takes on any input, and no","+  implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above","+  \\(2^n\\) does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises","+  nothing better than \\(2^n\\) there.","+\\item \\textbf{The constants \\(C, A, S, d, e\\) are existential and never","+  computed.} The comparator asserts their existence; the proof builds","+  them from existential lemmas (above, with \\(A \\ge 257\\) as","+  constructed) and states no value. The machine's state count is likewise","+  never computed.","+\\item \\textbf{With \\(A = 1\\), the verified exponent exceeds \\(n\\) for","+  every \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\).} That is Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}:","+  the exponential factor of the bound is at least \\(2^n\\) from 50","+  variables up to about \\(10^{2390}\\), and \\(2.17^{\\,n}\\)-ish at its","+  worst. With the true \\(A\\), whatever it is, the factor is","+  \\(2^{A\\rhon} \\ge 2^{\\rhon}\\) once \\(A \\ge 1\\), and the proof's own","+  construction has \\(A \\ge 257\\); so nothing is known that would put the","+  bound below \\(2^n\\) at any practical size.","+\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is","+  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all \\(2^n\\)","+  assignments and checking each against the \\(m\\) clauses counts the","+  models in \\(2^n \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) steps on any reasonable","+  machine, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is","+  \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) with \\(\\rhon > n\\) on \\(50 \\le n < e^{5504}\\)","+  and \\(C, A, d\\) unknown. The first bound is the smaller one on every","+  \\(n\\) in that range, whatever the unknown constants turn out to be,","+  because \\(A \\ge 1\\) already loses and \\(A < 1\\) is not what the proof","+  constructs.","+\\end{enumerate}","+","+None of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof; it is","+what an \\(O_k(1)\\) statement with a \\(2^{o(n)}\\) exponent of this shape","+means when the exponent's constants are not tracked. The manuscript","+itself calls \\(\\rhon\\) ``the weaker exponent'' (p.~2, line~63), and its","+sharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).","+One more detail of base: the solution's internal \\(\\rho\\)","+(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines 10--11) is in base 2 and","+is related to \\texttt{runtimeScale} by","+\\(\\mathtt{runtimeScale}\\ n \\le 4\\,\\rho_2(n)\\) for \\(n \\ge 20\\)","+(lines 189--190); the base changes the exponent by a constant factor","+that \\(A\\) absorbs, so nothing above depends on it.","+","+\\subsection{What the statement does establish}","+","+For each fixed \\(k \\ge 2\\), a deterministic machine that, on the canonical","+encoding of any \\(k\\)-CNF on \\(n\\) variables with \\(m\\) clauses, halts with","+the exact number of satisfying assignments in binary on a named tape,","+within \\(C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\) steps, where \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m)\\) is","+polynomial in \\(n + m\\) and \\(\\rhon = o(n)\\); and, because a","+\\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most \\(k\\) has at most","+\\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} = O(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at","+\\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), the whole bound is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone.","+That is a deterministic \\(2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)\\) algorithm","+for \\#\\(k\\)-SAT in the standard sense, and therefore for \\(k\\)-SAT; it","+refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001} as","+stated. The statement reads as the manuscript's verified form, the","+weaker exponent of its Proposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without","+the \\(n \\ge 20\\) proviso. We found no field, definition or quantifier","+through which the statement could be kernel-true and mean something","+weaker than this; the audit it summarises is","+\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement\\_audit.md}. Our own","+restatement of the statement as a Lean \\texttt{Prop}, character for","+character over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is","+\\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved and never an axiom there","+(Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).","+","+\\subsection{What this does not claim}","+","+\\begin{itemize}","+\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or","+  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any","+  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}; Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}","+  is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times","+  ordinary solvers, not this machine.","+\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means","+  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines; whether the proof of","+  it is sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay},","+  which found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, \\(A = 256 +","+  A_t\\), is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a","+  claim about the argument.","+\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} \\(A \\ge 257\\) is what the","+  proof writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of","+  the same statement could have any \\(A > 0\\). The statement itself","+  fixes nothing.","+\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which","+  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s subject.","+\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond \\(e^{5504}\\).} Past","+  \\(n \\approx 10^{2390}\\) the verified factor is below \\(2^n\\) at","+  \\(A = 1\\); we say nothing about that range except that it is not a","+  practical one.","+\\end{itemize}"]}],"originalFile":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02.\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\n\\placeholder{Item 02 drafts this section: the comparator's definitions one by one with their lines, the quantifier order, the encoding length, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$; the table (label \\texttt{tab:runtime-scale}) and figure (label \\texttt{fig:runtime-scale}) of $\\rhon/n$ (from \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}); the title's claim stated precisely. Ends with what this does not claim''.}\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015479o2UMCSeL9pFvKZeL7u","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
